          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-associated-vector-bundle-is-well-defined",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\pi:P\\to M$ be a smooth right principal $H$-bundle and let\n$\\rho:H\\to GL(V)$ be a smooth representation on a finite-dimensional real\nvector space. Then $P\\times_HV$ has a unique smooth rank-$\\dim V$ vector-bundle\nstructure over $M$ whose local trivializations are induced by principal-bundle\nsections. If $s_i=s_jg_{ji}$ on an overlap, the transition from the\n$i$-coordinates to the $j$-coordinates is $\\rho(g_{ji})$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-qftx-principal-connection-form",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, and let $\\pi:P\\to M$ be a smooth right principal $G$-bundle for a finite-dimensional real Lie group $G$, with $\\mathfrak g=\\operatorname{Lie}(G)=T_eG$. A supplied principal connection is a smooth, right-invariant complement $TP=VP\\oplus H$, where $VP=\\ker d\\pi$; write $v:TP\\to VP$ for the projection along $H$, and let $K_H(X,Y)=-v([hX,hY])$ be its vertical-valued curvature. For $Z\\in\\mathfrak g$ and $p\\in P$, define the fundamental vertical vector $Z_P(p)=\\left.\\frac{d}{dt}\\right|_{t=0}p\\exp_G(tZ)$. The orbit differential $\\zeta_p: \\mathfrak g\\to V_pP$, $Z\\mapsto Z_P(p)$, is a linear isomorphism, as proved below. Define the **principal connection 1-form** by",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-left-maurer-cartan-form",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ as in [[def-countable-choice]], let $G$ be a Lie\ngroup [[def-lie-group]] with identity $e$, and write $\\mathfrak g=T_eG$. A\n**$\\mathfrak g$-valued one-form** on $G$ means a\nsmooth map $\\alpha:TG\\to\\mathfrak g$ whose restriction\n$\\alpha_g:T_gG\\to\\mathfrak g$ is linear for every $g$. Equivalently,\n$V\\mapsto(\\pi_G(V),\\alpha(V))$ is a smooth vector-bundle map\n$TG\\to G\\times\\mathfrak g$ over $\\operatorname{id}_G$ in the sense of\n[[def-vector-bundle-map-over-a-smooth-base-map]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-differential-of-a-smooth-map",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $F:M\\to N$ be smooth and let $p\\in M$. The **differential of $F$ at $p$** is the map $dF_p:T_pM\\to T_{F(p)}N$ defined by\n$$ dF_p(v)([g]):=v([g\\circ F]) $$\nfor every derivation $v\\in T_pM$ and every germ $[g]\\in C_{F(p)}^\\infty(N)$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule-for-differentials-of-smooth-maps",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $F:M\\to N$ and $G:N\\to P$ are smooth, then $d(G\\circ F)_p=dG_{F(p)}\\circ dF_p$ for every $p\\in M$.",
