research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/invariant-review.md-17-I independently retrieved Brion's 2010 PDF at `https://ccirm.centre-mersenne.org/item/10.5802/ccirm.1.pdf` (HTTP 200, 678378 bytes, 23 PDF pages) and extracted/read printed pp. 7–9 using PyMuPDF, including Theorems 1.23 and 1.24 and their proofs. The failed `pdftotext` invocation reflects an unavailable executable; PyMuPDF extraction succeeded. Brion explicitly omits the deep compact-subgroup existence direction in Theorem 1.23. His Theorem 1.24 proves affine-target universality. I did not retrieve/read Milne, Popov–Vinberg, Dolgachev or the omitted chapter during this continuation, and do not adopt the interrupted session's unsaved source-access claims.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/invariant-review.md-18-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/invariant-review.md-19-## Supplier arguments
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/invariant-review.md-20-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/invariant-review.md:21:**Complete reducibility and Reynolds operator.** Steps 1.1–2.1 obtain complex Lie charts from smoothness and the Jacobian/holomorphic interfaces, and show the subgroup generated by exponentials is Zariski dense: its closure has full tangent dimension, and a smooth connected algebraic group is irreducible. Thus Lie-invariant subspaces are group-invariant by their algebraic stabilizers. In steps 2.2–3.1 the finite set of radical weights is fixed by connected conjugation, so `[g,rad(g)]` is strictly upper triangular. The closure generated by its polynomial exponential curves is connected, normal and unipotent in the exact fixed-vector definition; reductivity kills it. Levi then gives the central radical plus a semisimple summand.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/invariant-review.md-22-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/invariant-review.md-23-Step 5.1 does not assume that Jordan components already lie in the group Lie algebra: exponential-polynomial independence puts the exponential of the nilpotent component in the central Zariski closure. A nonzero component would give a closed normal additive unipotent subgroup. Hence the centre acts semisimply in the faithful embedding. Step 6.1 uses characters of the closed diagonal subgroup and the coaction to split arbitrary rational modules into its weights; Weyl splits each semisimple Lie-module weight space. Density lifts complements to the connected group. Step 7.1 justifies reductivity of the identity component by finite conjugates and successive fixed vectors, then averages a projection over the finite component group. Steps 8.1–9.1 supply arbitrary rational-module semisimplicity, the canonical trivial-isotypic projection, naturality, exactness on invariants, invariant scalar linearity and the recorded converses. The sum of nontrivial simple summands is the unique stable complement because equivariant maps from nontrivial simples to trivial modules vanish.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/invariant-review.md-24-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/invariant-review.md-25-Clause (iv), step 9.2, is explicitly conditional on a **given compact Zariski-dense subgroup**. Haar averaging, density and uniqueness give that same projection; the finite-dimensional convex-hull argument is valid. Compact existence is neither claimed nor used to establish clauses (i)–(iii). Consequently the unread compact-existence theorem is not a missing bridge here. No defect was found.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/invariant-review.md-26-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/invariant-review.md:27:**Reynolds ideal theory.** Each equality is justified by invariant linearity and naturality: `R(IA)=I`, contraction/extension injectivity, ascending-chain stabilization, and lifting an invariant through an equivariant surjection. Degree projections commute with the group, hence with Reynolds, so grading is preserved. The argument does not claim arbitrary ideals remain homogeneous or impose Noetherianity when it is absent. No defect was found.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/invariant-review.md-28-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/invariant-review.md:29:**Finite generation and affine categorical quotient.** The finite-dimensional lemma proves Noetherianity by Reynolds and finite generation by positive-degree induction; the affine embedding and equivariant invariant surjection transfer finite generation to every affine algebraic set. Step 2.1 proves invariant localization even with zero divisors by the localized Reynolds operator; it does not incorrectly assume the unlocalized ring injects into its localization. Maximal ideal extension plus Nullstellensatz proves surjectivity onto the classical affine model and closedness of its coordinate presentation. Step 3.1 uses Reynolds on the sum of stable ideals and a proper-ideal argument, avoiding the false identification of a sum of radical ideals with its radical. Closed-orbit existence and separation give exactly one closed orbit in each fibre. Domain and integral-closure arguments prove irreducibility and normality at the stated hypotheses.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/invariant-review.md-30-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/invariant-review.md-31-Step 5.1 supplies the stronger separated-classical-target universality locally, beyond Brion's affine-target formulation: an invariant map is constant on orbit closures, hence on each quotient fibre; the complement of an affine target chart pulls back to a stable closed set with closed image; invariant principal opens then descend its coordinates using localized invariants, and the local morphisms glue. Empty sets and the closed-point meaning of `Spec` are handled explicitly. No defect was found.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/invariant-review.md-32-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/invariant-review.md-33-## Hypotheses and choice
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/invariant-review.md-34-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/invariant-review.md-35-The supplier scope is complex **reductive affine algebraic groups**, finite-dimensional rational representations when finite-dimensionality is invoked, rational modules otherwise, algebraic actions on classical reduced affine algebraic sets, and commutative rational algebras for ring/ideal conclusions. Disconnected groups are allowed. This does not extend to arbitrary noncompact topological groups, positive characteristic, the full infinite-dimensional local gauge group, general nonaffine spaces, or unspecified infinite jet constructions. A categorical quotient parametrizes closed orbits; it need not be the orbit space or a geometric quotient.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/invariant-review.md-36-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/invariant-review.md:37:All three carrier statements explicitly assume AC. Carry full AC in consumers using this chain. Exact uses include inherited affine reconstruction/Nullstellensatz and maximal-ideal existence, the direct-sum-of-simples characterization in bridge step 8.1, Jordan decomposition's named assumption, and countable choice inherited by the Lie exponential/closed-subgroup interfaces. Haar and compact-complete-reducibility choice assumptions concern clause (iv) only. Finite component representatives need only finite choice. The elementary Reynolds identities themselves add no choice. A finite-dimensional quotient can be constructed with finite linear algebra, but that observation cannot erase assumptions of the imported chain actually used.
