+[Alonso, full HEFT geometry lecture notes](https://www.ippp.dur.ac.uk/~ralonso/PoHEFT.pdf)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** The finite coefficient model and all fields are those in the Statement, with smooth fields on the trivial bundle and the supplied gauge actions.
+
+[F1] [[post-aqft-smeft-model]]: $H$ is a smooth complex doublet of hypercharge $Y=1/2$ (integer charge $y=3$), and its conjugate is $\widetilde H=i\sigma^2H^*$; the adopted field content, unit conventions and direct-product gauge action are fixed there.
+
+[F2] [[post-aqft-heft-model]]: $h$ is gauge invariant with range $J=(-v,\infty)$, $U$ is a smooth $SU(2)$-valued field, $g_R(z)=\operatorname{diag}(z^3,z^{-3})$, and $U\mapsto g_LUg_R(z)^{-1}$; the local derivative is $D_\mu U=\partial_\mu U+igW_\mu U-ig'B_\mu UT_3$.
+
+[F3] [[def-aqft-warsaw-basis-contract]]: the selected finite list consists of the source-defined B- and L-conserving dimension-six polynomial representatives, with three-family components, at most six fields and the stated dimensionless coefficient convention; it does not claim a local completeness proof or compact-to-algebraic quotient identity.
+
+[F4] [[def-aqft-chiral-power-counting]]: the HEFT chiral/loop grading is separate from canonical dimension, and its retained NLO classes may have arbitrary smooth functions of $h/v$; no identity with a finite SMEFT dimension truncation is part of that definition.
+
+[F5] [[def-qftx-associated-covariant-derivative]] and [[lem-qftx-principal-curvature-cartan-bianchi]]: associated fields use the supplied right-principal connection and representation covariant derivative, and gauge curvature is $F_A=dA+\tfrac12[A\wedge A]$; in the electroweak doublet representation this gives the local $D_\mu H$ and the compatible $D_\mu U$ used above.
+
+[A1] [[def-axiom-of-choice]]: the exact selected reductive/Reynolds premise chain for the finite invariant-operator contract carries the full Axiom of Choice. It is retained as an inherited foundation for the coefficient contract; the pointwise substitution calculation below makes no choice.
+
+## Proof
+
+**Proof technique:** Construct the inverse on the nonzero-doublet patch, check gauge equivariance, and substitute finite polynomial representatives term by term.
+
+**Given:** [F1]–[F5] and [A1].
+
+1.1 For $H=(a,b)^T\ne0$, let $\rho=(H^\dagger H)^{1/2}>0$. Direct multiplication gives $\Sigma=(i\sigma^2H^*,H)=\begin{psmallmatrix}b^*&a\\-a^*&b\end{psmallmatrix}$, so $\Sigma^\dagger\Sigma=(|a|^2+|b|^2)I=\rho^2I$ and $\det\Sigma=|a|^2+|b|^2=\rho^2$. Therefore $U=\Sigma/\rho$ is unitary with determinant one, hence lies in $SU(2)$, and $h=\sqrt2\rho-v>-v$ lies in $J$. The inverse is smooth because $H\mapsto(H^\dagger H)^{1/2}$ is smooth on $\mathbb C^2\setminus\{0\}$ and division is only by $\rho>0$. Conversely, direct multiplication for an $SU(2)$ matrix gives $i\sigma^2U^*=Ui\sigma^2$ and $i\sigma^2e_2=e_1$, so $\Sigma(\Phi(h,U))=(v+h)U/\sqrt2$ and $\rho(\Phi(h,U))=(v+h)/\sqrt2$. These identities show that the two formulas compose to the identity in both directions. Thus $\Phi$ is a smooth diffeomorphism onto precisely the nonzero-doublet patch. [given, F1, F2, algebra]
+
+1.2 For $g_L\in SU(2)$, the defining two-dimensional representation obeys $i\sigma^2g_L^*=g_Li\sigma^2$; this follows by writing $g_L=\begin{psmallmatrix}a&b\\-b^*&a^*\end{psmallmatrix}$ and multiplying. Since $H\mapsto g_Lz^3H$, this identity gives $\widetilde H\mapsto g_Lz^{-3}\widetilde H$. Consequently $\Sigma\mapsto g_L\Sigma\operatorname{diag}(z^{-3},z^3)=g_L\Sigma g_R(z)^{-1}$. Unitarity preserves $\rho$, so $h$ is unchanged and $U=\Sigma/\rho$ has exactly the HEFT transformation law in [F2]. Hence $\Phi$ is equivariant. [given, F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra]
+
+1.3 The doublet derivative is $D_\mu H=\partial_\mu H+igW_\mu H+\tfrac{i}{2}g'B_\mu H$, because $Y(H)=1/2$. Applying the product rule to $H=(v+h)Ue_2/\sqrt2$ and using $T_3e_2=-e_2/2$ in the $D_\mu U$ formula of [F2] yields
+$$D_\mu H=\frac1{\sqrt2}\left((\partial_\mu h)Ue_2+(v+h)D_\mu Ue_2\right).$$
+The conjugate formula follows by complex conjugation. Repeated covariant derivatives expand into finite sums by the product rule, and field strengths remain the same curvature polynomials under this substitution. Thus every selected local polynomial representative has a finite HEFT expansion. [given, F1, F2, F5, algebra]
+
+1.4 Each undifferentiated $H$ or $\widetilde H$ in a representative contributes one factor $v+h$; each differentiated doublet contributes, by step 1.3, a sum whose terms contain at most one such factor. The source representatives contain at most six scalar fields by [F3]. Therefore every product term after the finite Leibniz expansion has radial degree at most six. Writing $x=h/v$ only extracts fixed powers of $v$ into its coefficient, so every radial coefficient is a polynomial in $x$ of degree at most six and hence analytic. The finite selected representatives produce finitely many HEFT monomials; define $R_{\alpha k,a}$ to be the coefficient of the $k$th radial power in monomial slot $\alpha$ after the stated substitution and dimensional normalization. Linearity of substitution then gives $f_{\alpha k}=\sum_aR_{\alpha k,a}c_a$, which is the claimed finite linear-image constraint. [given, F3, step 1.3, algebra]
+
+1.5 A degree-at-most-six polynomial has seventh derivative identically zero, whereas $(d/dx)^7e^x=e^x\ne0$ on every open interval. Thus $e^x$ cannot be one of the branch coefficient functions on any open interval. This exhibits a smooth HEFT coefficient choice outside this finite polynomial class and proves that the substitution does not parametrize general HEFT functions. [given, step 1.4, calculus]
+
+1.6 The finite starting list is selected by canonical dimension in [F3], whereas [F4] defines a separate chiral/loop grading and allows arbitrary smooth radial functions. No step identifies those gradings or their operator lists. The construction used only the nonzero-doublet diffeomorphism and finite substitutions; it supplies neither a value at $H=0$ nor an inverse map from arbitrary HEFT data, a compact-to-algebraic invariant-space equality, or a UV-completion theorem. [given, F3, F4, A1, step 1.1, step 1.4, scope] ∎
