The conjugate formula follows by complex conjugation. Repeated covariant derivatives expand into finite sums by the product rule, and field strengths remain the same curvature polynomials under this substitution. Thus every selected local polynomial representative has a finite HEFT expansion. [given, F1, F2, F5, algebra]

1.4 Each undifferentiated $H$ or $\widetilde H$ in a representative contributes one factor $v+h$; each differentiated doublet contributes, by step 1.3, a sum whose terms contain at most one such factor. The source representatives contain at most six scalar fields by [F3]. Therefore every product term after the finite Leibniz expansion has radial degree at most six. Writing $x=h/v$ only extracts fixed powers of $v$ into its coefficient, so every radial coefficient is a polynomial in $x$ of degree at most six and hence analytic. The finite selected representatives produce finitely many HEFT monomials; define $R_{\alpha k,a}$ to be the coefficient of the $k$th radial power in monomial slot $\alpha$ after the stated substitution and dimensional normalization. Linearity of substitution then gives $f_{\alpha k}=\sum_aR_{\alpha k,a}c_a$, which is the claimed finite linear-image constraint. [given, F3, step 1.3, algebra]

1.5 A degree-at-most-six polynomial has seventh derivative identically zero, whereas $(d/dx)^7e^x=e^x\ne0$ on every open interval. Thus $e^x$ cannot be one of the branch coefficient functions on any open interval. This exhibits a smooth HEFT coefficient choice outside this finite polynomial class and proves that the substitution does not parametrize general HEFT functions. [given, step 1.4, calculus]

1.6 The finite starting list is selected by canonical dimension in [F3], whereas [F4] defines a separate chiral/loop grading and allows arbitrary smooth radial functions. No step identifies those gradings or their operator lists. The construction used only the nonzero-doublet diffeomorphism and finite substitutions; it supplies neither a value at $H=0$ nor an inverse map from arbitrary HEFT data, a compact-to-algebraic invariant-space equality, or a UV-completion theorem. [given, F3, F4, A1, step 1.1, step 1.4, scope] ∎
**Proof technique:** Evaluate the selected operator in radial gauge, integrate by parts, and normalize the scalar coordinate locally at the vacuum.

**Given:** [F1]–[F4] and [A1]–[A4].

1.1 In the stated unitary-gauge configuration, $H^\dagger H=(v+h)^2/2$. Its d'Alembertian is $\Box(H^\dagger H)=\partial_\mu((v+h)\partial^\mu h)$. Therefore the selected operator is the product $(v+h)^2\partial_\mu h\partial^\mu h/2+(v+h)^3\Box h/2$ before integration by parts; equivalently, writing $s=H^\dagger H$, it is $s\Box s$. [given, F2, F3, algebra]

1.2 Integrating $s\Box s$ over spacetime and applying the divergence theorem to $\partial_\mu(s\partial^\mu s)$ gives $\int s\Box s=-\int\partial_\mu s\partial^\mu s$, since the boundary integral vanishes by [A2]. Here $\partial_\mu s=(v+h)\partial_\mu h$, so the action contribution is $-C_{H\Box}\int (v+h)^2\partial_\mu h\partial^\mu h/\Lambda_{\mathrm{op}}^2$. The sign uses the stated $\Box$ and mostly-plus contraction. [given, A2, step 1.1, integration by parts]

1.3 The renormalizable radial kinetic density is $-\tfrac12\partial_\mu h\partial^\mu h$. Adding the result of step 1.2 gives $-\tfrac12K(h)\partial_\mu h\partial^\mu h$ with $K(h)=1+2C_{H\Box}(v+h)^2/\Lambda_{\mathrm{op}}^2$. Since $C_{H\Box}$ is dimensionless and both $v+h$ and $\Lambda_{\mathrm{op}}$ have mass dimension one, $K$ is dimensionless. Under [A3], $K>0$ near zero. [given, F1, F2, A3, step 1.2, algebra]

1.4 With $U=I$, write $r=v+h$ and use $T_a=\sigma_a/2$. The upper component of the covariant derivative is $(D_\mu H)_1=ig r(W_\mu^1-iW_\mu^2)/(2\sqrt2)=ig rW_\mu^+/2$. Since $W^1,W^2$ are real, its norm squared is $g^2r^2W_\mu^-W^{+\mu}/4$. The Higgs kinetic term therefore contains $-g^2r^2W_\mu^+W^{-\mu}/4$. The chosen $Q_{H\Box}$ has no gauge field, so this coefficient is unchanged as a function of $h$; thus $m_W^2(h)=g^2(v+h)^2/4$ and $m_W^2(0)=g^2v^2/4$. [given, A1, F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra]

1.5 On an interval with $K>0$, $\widehat h'(h)=\sqrt{K(h)}>0$, so $\widehat h$ is strictly increasing and is a local radial coordinate. Its derivative at zero is $\sqrt{K(0)}$. The difference quotient for $m_W^2$ as a function of this coordinate is therefore $\left.dm_W^2/d\widehat h\right|_0=(dm_W^2/dh|_0)/\sqrt{K(0)}=(g^2v/2)/\sqrt{K(0)}$. Since $2m_W^2(0)/v=g^2v/2$, the defined coupling is $\kappa_W=1/\sqrt{K(0)}$, which yields the displayed expression. If this reference point is called a vacuum, the supplied potential must satisfy [F5]; otherwise the result is only a local expansion coefficient. It supplies no empirical measurement or numerical bound on discarded orders. [given, F4, F5, A1, A3, A4, step 1.3, step 1.4, calculus] ∎
